Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program that uses advanced features such as async/await, Promises, destructuring, and template literals to fetch and display data from a mocked API:

```javascript
// Mock an API call
const fetchData = () => {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      resolve({
        users: [
          { id: 1, name: 'Alice', age: 28 },
          { id: 2, name: 'Bob', age: 32 },
          { id: 3, name: 'Charlie', age: 25 }
        ]
      });
    }, 1000);
  });
};

// Async function to fetch and process data
async function processData() {
  try {
    const { users } = await fetchData();
    
    const getUserInfo = (user) => {
      const { name, age } = user;
      return `Name: ${name}, Age: ${age}`;
    };
    
    users.forEach((user) => console.log(getUserInfo(user)));

    const averageAge = users.reduce((acc, { age }) => acc + age, 0) / users.length;
    console.log(`Average Age: ${averageAge.toFixed(2)}`);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
  }
}

 
processData();
```

This code demonstrates several JavaScript features and concepts in a cohesive manner, such as using async/await for handling asynchronous operations, destructuring objects to access properties easily, and template literals for string interpolation.